From 3fe7ae976dc95d59e7cec1a93143a81cc16347af Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Zenium Upstream Port <upstream-port@zenium.invalid>
Date: Sat, 5 Sep 2026 08:01:30 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 80/82] misc/mediatek/m4u: port the MT6768 M4U v2.0 engine
 without ION

The SMI substrate in 0078/0079 exists for this driver, so M4U moves now: 16 files and
10,896 lines from drivers/misc/mediatek/m4u/{2.0,mt6768} of the 4.19 tree, built obj-y
under a new CONFIG_MTK_M4U.  Everything is byte-identical to the vendor source except
where 5.15 forces a change, and each such place carries a "Port note (5.15)" comment
naming the version that moved.

The BSP's Kconfig line is `MTK_M4U: depends on MTK_ION`, and it cannot be carried rather
than being chosen against: `grep -rn "config MTK_ION"` over 5.15.220 returns nothing, so
that dependency would leave MTK_M4U unreachable no matter what else is enabled.  It is
also not M4U's dependency.  M4U's only ION code is m4u_test_ion(), inside
`#ifdef CONFIG_M4U_TEST_ION`, a symbol the BSP defines in no Kconfig and even_defconfig
does not set, and m4u_alloc_mva() takes a VA (the driver builds the scatterlist itself
with vmalloc_to_page()/follow_pte()) or the caller's sg_table under M4U_FLAGS_SG_READY.
What M4U does depend on is the vendor SMI API - its larb keeps are
`#ifdef CONFIG_MTK_SMI_EXT`, and 0079 renamed the SMI symbol precisely so this driver
would not compile them into smi_public.h's `((void)0)` fallback - so the new Kconfig
says `depends on MTK_SMI_EXT`.  No ION subsystem is transplanted and CONFIG_DMABUF_HEAPS
stays off: no ported client has asked for a buffer source yet, and when display/video
lands, dma_buf_get/attach/map_attachment_unlocked hands over exactly the sg_table M4U
already accepts.

Everything else the config stack looked fatal is config, not code: aee.h (there is no
aee_*() call in any built M4U file - m4u_aee_err() is a local snprintf+pr_debug macro,
and CONFIG_MTK_AEE_FEATURE is unset in even_defconfig), the whole TEE/Trustonic/
GenieZone surface including m4u_sec_gp.c and tz_m4u.h (needs CONFIG_MTK_TEE_GP_SUPPORT
plus SEC_VIDEO_PATH/CAM_SECURITY, none of which exist here), CONFIG_FPGA_EARLY_PORTING,
and the ION test block in m4u_debug.c.  M4U_PROFILE *is* defined unconditionally by
mt6768/m4u_priv.h:89, but every use of it is inside `#ifdef M4U_PROFILE`, and the BSP's
own mmp/mmprofile.h provides a `!CONFIG_MMPROFILE` branch of no-op inlines for exactly
this case ("Put dummy API implementation here"), so the three mmp API headers are carried
and mmp/src is not: M4U's six trace events are compiled out rather than faked, which is
written down as a gap.  sync_write.h and mt-plat/mtk_lpae.h (empty unless
CONFIG_MTK_LM_MODE) come along verbatim so that no #include line in the vendored code had
to be rewritten; the new Makefile's -I list is just the BSP's own set of directories.

Six 5.15 API adaptations, from one line to a few:
 - struct proc_ops replaces file_operations for /proc nodes (v5.6).  That is the
   vendor's DEFINE_PROC_ATTRIBUTE macro (nine /proc/m4u_dbg/* attribute nodes), six
   hand-written m4u_proc_*_fops in m4u_debug.c/m4u_pgtable.c, and a proc twin of
   m4u_fops for /proc/m4u.  5.15's simple_attr_open() still reads inode->i_private, so
   the macro's local_inode copy stays; generic_file_llseek becomes default_llseek.
   proc_ops has no flush callback while MTK_M4U_flush() is a vendor no-op, and it does
   have proc_ioctl/proc_compat_ioctl, so /proc/m4u keeps its ioctls.
 - platform_driver lost .suspend/.resume.  m4u_pm_ops (->driver.pm) already runs
   m4u_suspend()/m4u_resume() through m4u_pm_suspend/m4u_pm_resume and 5.15's dev_pm_ops
   still has the members that initialiser uses, so only the two dead fields go away and
   the register backup/restore path is untouched.
 - m4u_user_v2p()'s walk gains the folded p4d level; on arm64 p4d_offset(pgd, va) *is*
   the pgd, so the added test can only fail where pgd_bad() would have.
 - handle_mm_fault() gained the faulting pt_regs; the one call passes NULL.
 - do_mmap_pgoff() -> do_mmap(), same eight arguments.
 - show_pte() is static in 5.15's arch/arm64/mm/fault.c, so the two calls that dumped a
   user PTE after a failed follow take a comment instead: m4u_user_v2p() directly above
   reports which level rejected the address.  This was the round's only link failure
   (ld.lld: error: undefined symbol: show_pte); what is lost is the raw pte value, not
   the diagnosis.

One vendor defect is kept on purpose.  m4u_hw.c:1723 reads
`if ((port_array->ports[port] && M4U_PORT_ATTR_EN) == 0)`, where a bit test was meant,
and clang says so (-Wtautological-constant-compare).  Correcting it would change which
M4U ports get programmed on a shipping SoC, in a commit whose job is to move code.

Verified on build-36 with the SMI round's tools: 0 errors, and no warning from this
directory other than that inherited line; objects 7372 -> 7377; Image
26,966,024 -> 27,035,656; Image.gz-dtb 11,141,946.  vmlinux exports m4u_alloc_mva,
m4u_alloc_mva_sg, m4u_hw_init, m4u_reg_init, m4u_pgtable_init and m4u_debug_init, with
M4U's SMI calls resolving to drivers/memory/mtk-smi-mt6768.c.  Bindings were checked
against the DTB the image ships, because m4u_reg_init() does its own
of_find_compatible_node() lookups: mediatek,smi_common (M4U_MMU_SLAVE_SWITCH is on for
MT6768) and mediatek,smi_larb0..4 from gM4U_SMILARB[] all resolve to nodes present in
mt6768.dtb, whose sha256 is unchanged (34a7e6b5...85a11cd) - no DT conversion to mainline
IOMMU bindings, no node deleted.  hwenable.py's table moves by exactly one row,
mediatek,m4u NO_DRIVER -> ENABLED (bound 33 -> 34, enabled 24 -> 25), and clkaudit.py
--require-fresh is unchanged at 234 refs / 234 registered / 0 unresolved: M4U never
calls clk_get, so the node's clocks cell stays with its owner as on stock.  boot.img
repacked at 11,268,096 B, its dtb section byte-identical to the built .dtb.

Out of scope, and written down rather than stubbed: MTK's extended-ION multimedia
allocation ABI the clients want (ION_CMD_MULTIMEDIA booking, ion_mm_data,
ION_LOG_*/DECOUPLE_*/GAINCONTROL, ion_phys, ion_map_kernel, /dev/ion), the MMP trace
buffer, the TEE secure path, and 32-bit M4U ioctls - the last because this base carries
no fs/compat.c at all, so the port selects the vendor's own MTK_M4U_COMPAT_ioctl == NULL
branch instead of inventing a compat stack allocator.
